$article=['date'=>'2026-12-12','slug'=>'bilingual-homes-speech-delay','tag'=>'Speech & Language','category'=>'Speech & Language Development','breadcrumb'=>'Bilingual Speech Delay','meta_title'=>'Bilingual Speech Delay: Do Two Languages Cause It?','description'=>'Bilingual homes do not cause speech delay. Learn what typical multilingual development can look like and how a proper assessment examines every language.','h1'=>'Bilingual Homes and Speech Delay: Is Raising a Child in Two Languages the Cause?','dek'=>'Hindi and English do not confuse a child&rsquo;s brain. The right assessment looks at the whole communication system.','body'=><<<'HTML'
<p class="text-lg text-gray-700 leading-relaxed font-light">Bilingual speech delay is a misleading phrase when it suggests that hearing two languages causes disorder. Children can learn more than one language. Their vocabulary may be distributed, so measuring only English can underestimate what they know.</p><h2>What multilingual development can look like</h2><p>A child may know one word in Hindi and another in English, mix languages within a sentence or use different languages with different people. Code-switching is not evidence of confusion.</p><h2>Difference versus disorder</h2><p>A language difference reflects the languages and models a child has experienced. A disorder affects learning and using language across the child&rsquo;s languages, although it may look different in each. Limited exposure must not be scored as inability.</p><h2>What should an assessment include?</h2><p>The speech-language pathologist should document when, where and with whom each language is used; gather parent and teacher information; assess communication across languages where possible; and use interpreters, language samples or dynamic assessment appropriately. Monolingual norms require caution.</p><h2>Should families stop one language?</h2><p>Usually no. ASHA encourages families to communicate in the languages they are most comfortable using. Rich, natural interaction supports connection and learning better than a parent restricting themselves to less fluent language.</p><h2>When to seek help</h2><p>Raise concerns when understanding, gestures or spoken language are limited across languages, progress stalls or communication affects daily life. Hearing should also be considered. Explore <a href="/speech-therapy/language-delay">language delay assessment</a>.</p>
HTML,'faqs'=>[['Does speaking Hindi and English cause speech delay?','No. Learning multiple languages does not cause a speech or language disorder. Vocabulary should be considered across languages rather than judging English words alone.'],['Is mixing two languages a warning sign?','No. Code-switching is a normal multilingual behaviour. Concern depends on the child&rsquo;s broader ability to understand, learn and communicate across their languages.'],['Should we use only English before school starts?','Families should generally use languages they speak naturally and confidently. Strong interaction and connection matter, and maintaining the home language supports family relationships and learning.'],['How is a bilingual child assessed fairly?','Assessment should consider every language, exposure, cultural context and appropriate comparison data, using parent report, language samples, interpreters or dynamic assessment when needed.']],
